r any  of our people  and we have  activated the alternative routes and  sources um so as i said since february  we've been ready for this moment we were  hoping it would not come in that russia  downwards contracts but it came  and we haven't had any interruption in  supply we don't expect any interruption  in supply uh and as i said um about a  month ago we pushed for a common  european purchasing strategy on gas  because for lmg in those quantities you  need careful scheduling and large scale  deliveries  bulgaria has done its own  supply reservations in the lmg terminals  as well as all the pipelines that we  need to get the gas from greece and 32  bulgaria  but  in addition to our three billion cubic  meters we can  take supply from the southern gas  corridor as well as lng terminals in  greece and turkey to the tune of about  20 billion cubic meters that can then go  to  romania and from there to the rest of  europe or go to serbia  hungary and then to the rest of europe  we've even had  discussions  sorry i didn't want to talk across you  um the the kremlin has released a  statement saying that you know it's a  simple matter of bulgaria paying in  rubles this is what they have asked for  all along you know they are a reliable  energy supplier and it doesn't engage in  blackmail your response to that  it's a simple matter of following the  contract  the contract says we pay in u.s dollars  in a specific account in gazprom bank  any change to the contract done  unilaterally regardless of how nicely  it's requested or how nicely it's not  requested  visa change of contract which the other  party needs to agree  we haven't agreed to that change of  contract  which means that russia is now breaking  the contract and we will obviously go to  international trash court  to seek for damages  why do you think bulgaria  has been targeted in this way  specifically  uh i don't think bulgaria is being  targeted uh poland bulgaria and my  understanding is lithuania were the  first countries  that had payments due to  gas problem  i think the other countries that are  coming up with payments if they follow  the strategy of  paying exactly as per contract and not  accepting unilateral contract changes  would probably face the same situation  do you think it might be because  bulgaria is involved in providing  military equipment to ukraine  i don't think there is a link between  the two  we've seen at for example turkey  providing very vital military equipment  to ukraine and we haven't heard about  any gas supplies getting cut to turkey  would you consider paying in rubles to  russia i mean how long can you  carry on with these alternative methods  for  forever uh no we will not play in rurals  what you seem pretty frustrated and  angry in in the way russia has dealt  with this situation  well i think we've had bilateral  relations that are constructive and  what we've seen is a unilateral breach  of contract  that obviously doesn't make us happy i  wouldn't say we're angry we were  prepared for that because we've had  similar incidents in 2009 uh 2014 um so  we  were one of the first in the european  union to get the to push for contingency  plans for deliveries we've made our  contingency plans our gas from contracts  expires at the end of this year anyways  so  basically what we see going forward is  zero russian gas in our economy  while i have you i'd like to ask you  about  what you feel about the situation on the  ground in ukraine what's happening and  where this is all headed  um it's a terrible human tragedy that  we're seeing the fact that we have uh  targeting of civilians uh i think is  um  beyond belief uh and  something which cannot be justified with  any rhetoric  on on the russian side  we support the ukraine firmly we support  all the sanctions we are pushing for  strict sanctions to be imposed on russia  and um we think that uh right now  there is a lot of focus that is done on  delivering equipment which is very  important for ukraine um i think also we  need to start thinking and planning  ahead for the incoming winter  where there will need to be a lot of  logistical work that needs to be done  making sure there is sufficient food  supply making sure ukraine can receive  sufficient gas to heat its people and  keep its economy going  making sure that all the logistical  corridors work  all of those are critical matters that  if we wait until september october or  november would be too late we need to  start preparing for that now and we  actually had some conversations with the  ukrainian minister of finance and how we  might be able to help there  we have a delegation led by our prime  minister uh going to meet with mr  zelensky tomorrow which is focused  exactly on those issues  beyond the military conflict and weapons  which seem pretty much at hand at this  point uh how can we move to the next  part which is making sure the ukrainian  economy is stable and that the incoming  winter which is going to be very tough  uh regardless of when the war ends  is properly planned for and that the  ukrainians have all the food all the  energy supplies  and all the existing infrastructure  logistics wise to be able to  make sure that their people are  actually cared for  deputy prime minister asan vasilev  thanks very much indeed for speaking to  us here on sky news thank you
 

